namespace Eigen {

/** \page StructuredMatricesExamples StructuredMatrices examples

These programs solve problems from numerical PDEs, integral equations, approximation theory, statistics, control and
quantum mechanics with the \ref StructuredMatrices_Module. Each one replaces a dense or sparse factorization by an
algorithm that exploits a structure of the problem -- a Kronecker product or sum, Toeplitz, Hankel or circulant,
Vandermonde, Cauchy, or diagonal plus low rank -- and checks its result against a known solution or a general-purpose
solver. The bracketed numbers cite the module's
\ref StructuredMatricesReferences "references".

\tableofcontents

\section StructuredExamplesGrids Separable operators on tensor-product grids

Finite differences on a tensor-product grid give Kronecker sums of 1-D matrices: with grid values stored with x as the
fast index, the 2-D Laplacian is \f$ D_y \oplus D_x = D_y \otimes I + I \otimes D_x \f$. \ref KroneckerSum applies
such operators without forming them, and \ref BartelsStewart solves them through the Schur forms or
eigendecompositions of the 1-D factors. LeVeque [1] covers the discretizations.

\subsection StructuredExamplePoisson2D The 2-D Poisson equation

The fast Poisson solver: two \f$ n \times n \f$ eigendecompositions instead of a factorization of the
\f$ n^2 \times n^2 \f$ matrix, with the second-order convergence of the discretization: the fast diagonalization
method of Lynch, Rice and Thomas [2].

<table class="example">
<tr><th>Example:</th><th>Output:</th></tr>
<tr><td>
\include StructuredPoisson2D.cpp
</td>
<td>
\verbinclude StructuredPoisson2D.out
</td></tr></table>

\subsection StructuredExampleHeat3D Implicit time stepping of the 3-D heat equation

The decompositions are computed once and reused at every step. A shift such as the identity of
\f$ I + \tau L \f$ goes into one factor, since \f$ I \otimes I \otimes I = I \f$.

<table class="example">
<tr><th>Example:</th><th>Output:</th></tr>
<tr><td>
\include StructuredHeat3D.cpp
</td>
<td>
\verbinclude StructuredHeat3D.out
</td></tr></table>

\subsection StructuredExampleConvectionDiffusion Convection-diffusion

Nonsymmetric 1-D operators take the Schur-form path of \ref BartelsStewart, the algorithm of Bartels and Stewart [3];
the solution agrees with \c SparseLU on the assembled matrix.

<table class="example">
<tr><th>Example:</th><th>Output:</th></tr>
<tr><td>
\include StructuredConvectionDiffusion.cpp
</td>
<td>
\verbinclude StructuredConvectionDiffusion.out
</td></tr></table>

\subsection StructuredExampleADI Alternating-direction implicit time stepping

The Peaceman-Rachford ADI method [4] treats one direction at a time. Each half step is a \ref KroneckerOperator with an
\c Identity() factor, which costs nothing: the product is a sparse product with the 1-D matrix and the solve one
\c SparseLU of a tridiagonal matrix.

<table class="example">
<tr><th>Example:</th><th>Output:</th></tr>
<tr><td>
\include StructuredADI.cpp
</td>
<td>
\verbinclude StructuredADI.out
</td></tr></table>

\subsection StructuredExamplePreconditioner A non-separable potential

When a term breaks the separable structure, the separable part still helps twice: \ref KroneckerSparseView
assembles the sparse matrix in one expression, and the fast Poisson solver preconditions conjugate gradients.

<table class="example">
<tr><th>Example:</th><th>Output:</th></tr>
<tr><td>
\include StructuredPreconditioner.cpp
</td>
<td>
\verbinclude StructuredPreconditioner.out
</td></tr></table>

\subsection StructuredExampleTorusHelmholtz Periodic boundaries: the screened Poisson equation on a torus

With periodic boundaries the five-point stencil is a block circulant with circulant blocks, solved by \ref Bccb
through the 2-D FFT [5]. It is also the Kronecker sum of two 1-D periodic second-difference circulants, and the two
solvers agree.

<table class="example">
<tr><th>Example:</th><th>Output:</th></tr>
<tr><td>
\include StructuredTorusHelmholtz.cpp
</td>
<td>
\verbinclude StructuredTorusHelmholtz.out
</td></tr></table>

\section StructuredExamplesSpectra Matrix equations and spectra

\subsection StructuredExampleLyapunov The Lyapunov equation of a mass-spring chain

\f$ A X + X A^T = C \f$ is \f$ (A \oplus A)\,\mathrm{vec}(X) = \mathrm{vec}(C) \f$, and the Sylvester equation
\f$ A X + X B = C \f$ is \f$ (B^T \oplus A)\,\mathrm{vec}(X) = \mathrm{vec}(C) \f$. This example computes the
controllability Gramian of a damped chain, whose system matrix has complex eigenvalue pairs; Simoncini [6] surveys the
methods for such matrix equations.

<table class="example">
<tr><th>Example:</th><th>Output:</th></tr>
<tr><td>
\include StructuredLyapunov.cpp
</td>
<td>
\verbinclude StructuredLyapunov.out
</td></tr></table>

\subsection StructuredExampleHarmonicOscillator The 2-D quantum harmonic oscillator

A separable Hamiltonian \f$ H_x \oplus H_y \f$ has the pairwise sums of the 1-D energies as its spectrum, which
\ref KroneckerSum::eigenvalues returns without forming the 2-D matrix [7].

<table class="example">
<tr><th>Example:</th><th>Output:</th></tr>
<tr><td>
\include StructuredHarmonicOscillator.cpp
</td>
<td>
\verbinclude StructuredHarmonicOscillator.out
</td></tr></table>

\subsection StructuredExampleImpurity An impurity bound state

A localized perturbation of a diagonal band Hamiltonian is a rank-one update, the Koster-Slater model [8]. \ref DPR1EigenSolver finds the whole
spectrum in \f$ O(N^2) \f$ from the secular equation [9], including the bound state that splits off below the band.

<table class="example">
<tr><th>Example:</th><th>Output:</th></tr>
<tr><td>
\include StructuredImpurity.cpp
</td>
<td>
\verbinclude StructuredImpurity.out
</td></tr></table>

\section StructuredExamplesToeplitz Toeplitz, circulant and Hankel systems

\subsection StructuredExampleLoveEquation Love's integral equation and a preconditioned Toeplitz system

A translation-invariant kernel discretized on a uniform grid gives a \ref Toeplitz matrix. Love's equation [10] is solved
directly by \ref LookAheadLevinson [11] in \f$ O(n^2) \f$. For large systems, conjugate gradients use the FFT-based
Toeplitz product and Strang's \ref Circulant preconditioner [12], [13], \f$ O(n \log n) \f$ per iteration.

<table class="example">
<tr><th>Example:</th><th>Output:</th></tr>
<tr><td>
\include StructuredLoveEquation.cpp
</td>
<td>
\verbinclude StructuredLoveEquation.out
</td></tr></table>

\subsection StructuredExamplePade Pade approximation

The denominator of a Pade approximant [14] solves a \ref Hankel system in the Taylor coefficients, solved in
\f$ O(n^2) \f$ through its Toeplitz equivalent. Rescaling the variable equilibrates the coefficients and keeps the
system Hankel.

<table class="example">
<tr><th>Example:</th><th>Output:</th></tr>
<tr><td>
\include StructuredPade.cpp
</td>
<td>
\verbinclude StructuredPade.out
</td></tr></table>

\section StructuredExamplesApproximation Interpolation and approximation

\subsection StructuredExampleQuadrature Interpolatory quadrature

The weights of an interpolatory quadrature rule solve the transposed \ref Vandermonde system, the dual problem that
\ref BjorckPereyra [16] solves in \f$ O(n^2) \f$; on Chebyshev extreme points they are the Clenshaw-Curtis weights
[15]. The primal solve gives interpolation coefficients.

<table class="example">
<tr><th>Example:</th><th>Output:</th></tr>
<tr><td>
\include StructuredQuadrature.cpp
</td>
<td>
\verbinclude StructuredQuadrature.out
</td></tr></table>

\subsection StructuredExampleRationalApproximation A discretized Cauchy integral

Representing an analytic function by point charges outside its domain leads to a \ref Cauchy system, factored with
pivoting in \f$ O(n^2) \f$ by \ref CauchyLU [18]; the error decays geometrically, as for the trapezoidal rule on a
circle [17].

<table class="example">
<tr><th>Example:</th><th>Output:</th></tr>
<tr><td>
\include StructuredRationalApproximation.cpp
</td>
<td>
\verbinclude StructuredRationalApproximation.out
</td></tr></table>

\section StructuredExamplesStatistics Statistics

\subsection StructuredExampleGaussianProcess A Gaussian-process likelihood

A covariance that is noise plus a low-rank feature model [19], [20] is a \ref DiagonalPlusLowRank operator. Its solve
(Woodbury identity) and determinant (matrix determinant lemma) cost \f$ O(n k^2) \f$, the price of evaluating the
log-likelihood.

<table class="example">
<tr><th>Example:</th><th>Output:</th></tr>
<tr><td>
\include StructuredGaussianProcess.cpp
</td>
<td>
\verbinclude StructuredGaussianProcess.out
</td></tr></table>

